\DOC WINDOWS_X86_WRAP_STACK_TAC

\TYPE {WINDOWS_X86_WRAP_STACK_TAC : thm -> thm -> thm -> term -> int -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Proves a Windows-ABI {ensures} specification for a stack-using wrapper routine
from the corresponding standard-ABI core specification, either closing the goal
outright or reducing it to a residual event-trace obligation.

\DESCRIBE
The arguments are {winmc : thm}, the definition of the Windows wrapper's
machine code, matching the code loaded in the goal; {stdmc : thm}, the
definition of the standard-ABI core machine code; {coreth : thm}, the
already-proved specification of the core routine (a correctness {ensures}
statement, or an existentially quantified event-trace/safety statement);
{reglist : term}, a HOL list of the callee-saved registers the wrapper spills
(possibly empty); and {stdstackoff : int}, the stack offset used by the
standard-ABI core. It applies to a goal with no assumptions whose conclusion is
a universally quantified Windows-ABI {ensures} statement, optionally preceded
by an existential quantifier over an event-trace function. The tactic either
closes the goal or leaves one subgoal, whose conclusion {?x1 ... xn. u} is a
purely event-trace statement relating a concrete explicit trace (stores of
saved registers, the core routine's trace, loads and a final jump) to the
required trace function, and whose assumption list {A} is created from nothing,
containing: the nonoverlapping side conditions in unfolded
{nonoverlapping_modulo} form, the core routine's trace and {memaccess_inbounds}
facts, and facts about a final concrete state — code loaded, register and flag
values, saved values in stack slots, program counter and stack pointer, and a
{MAYCHANGE} frame relating the initial and final states.
{
            ?- t
   =================================  WINDOWS_X86_WRAP_STACK_TAC winmc stdmc coreth reglist stdstackoff
      (no goals)   or   A ?- ?x1 ... xn. u
}

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only these two outcomes, always starting from goals
with no assumptions.

\ENDDOC
